{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection","id":1550014,"title":"Reflexion about armor protection","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-04-10T15:43:46-07:00","id":1550014,"title":"Reflexion about armor protection","content":"According to V20, W20 and M20 an armor give the character extra soaking dice when suffering damage. When the character isn't supernaturally tough those dice are the only one they can use to soak lethal from guns, rifles and other similar threats<br /> <br /> Let's focus on firearms and use a mere humans with no power at all. A vile murderer shoot him with a gun (a classic 9mm). Considering how a body armor such a skevlar work, it should prevent the bullet from penetrating the body, leaving only the impact kick the poor guy. Since the bullet didn't go through then there's no hole in the skin, no muscle and soft tissues torn appart and no bone shattered, meaning that if the victim suffer damage it's going to be bashing and not lethal, allowing him to soak them with it's stamina<br /> <br /> Problem: after some research it seems that if soft armor can protect against pistols, when it come to rifle it's another story. From my reading most of those rifles round use hard material to get through armor, wich is why most bulletproof vest used in army are built with hard plate that can stop these kind of bullet.<br /> It means that a soft body armor protect you from most small caliber (mostly pistols) while hard body armor protect you from bigger caliber such as rifles. And since there's 6 levels of protection in body armor it's even more complex that just soft and hard<br /> <br /> If you were to change something in armor functioning, what would you do?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-10T17:11:25-07:00","id":1550015,"title":"","content":"I don't think you can really fix armor without overhauling the entire combat system.<br /> <br /> Though a quick fix is to copy the CofD 2e combat rules: damage and &quot;soak&quot; is unrolled, weapon damage adds to final damage, armor has two potential effects. Armor can reduce final damage or downgrade some lethal damage to bashing damage.<br /> <br /> So, in CofD2e, a Kevlar vest is rated 1/3 (general/ballistic). The first three lethal damage from guns is reduced to bashing damage, and the total damage (starting with the worst damage) is reduced by 1. So if you get 5 damage from a gun, you end up taking 1 lethal and 3 bashing.<br /> <br /> It's far from realistic, but it has a better feel towards armor being significant without making combat a slog by making characters too tanky as armor might make gunfights less deadly but normal humans are still going to fall quickly to all the bashing damage (and can just be executed while unconscious if the enemy wants to) to make sure violence is still very much a threat (though the ST doesn't have to make the enemy so ruthless).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-11T02:27:07-07:00","id":1550028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550015#post1550015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think you can really fix armor without overhauling the entire combat system.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure an entire overhauling would be necessary, sorting protection according to different level of penetration could do just fine to determine if the lethal damage are converted into bashing.<br /> <br /> A level 1 protection (heavy clothing, and other material that are not capable of stopping bullet) does not convert damage from stabbing weapons and firearms of any kind<br /> A level 2 protection (Kevlar and soft armor without any hard plate) does convert lethal from low-powered weapon (pistols and such), but does not convert stabbing weapons and more powerfull weapons (rifles like m16 and ak47) because they lack hard plate to stop them<br /> A level 3 protection (military outfit and hard armor) does convert lethal from powerfull weapons and stabbing weapons, only high powered weapons (big caliber like .50 and high velocity weapons like machine gun) aren't converted<br /> Of course it's not as complexe as real life's 6 level of protection but it uses their operating principle as working rule<br /> <br /> pc and npc can always make a precise shot to avoid that protection, and accumulation of bashing damage can still knock out, so i don't think there's so much to change in the game if the st want to use that system","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-11T02:47:41-07:00","id":1550030,"title":"","content":"So... what about stab plates and modern chain that's designed to reduce the threat of shrapnel?<br /> <br /> As well... what counts as a stabbing weapon and what doesn't? Where's the line between low and high powered weapons?<br /> <br /> Is conversion total or partial?<br /> <br /> How are you going to compensate for the increased incentive to use targeted shots to circumvent armor as you make armor stronger, as currently those penalties aren't that high as called shots aren't optimal for dealing damage and more oriented around secondary effects?<br /> <br /> The WoD combat system is complicated. If you change one part, you impact dozens of others (and even more once you start looking at supernatural powers). That's why I said a full fix would take a lot more work, but still offered something to consider as a patch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-11T14:44:25-07:00","id":1550060,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550030#post1550030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So... what about stab plates and modern chain that's designed to reduce the threat of shrapnel?</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say that it does not convert explosion damage since shrapnels can fly in every direction including non armored part of the character<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550030#post1550030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> As well... what counts as a stabbing weapon and what doesn't? Where's the line between low and high powered weapons?</div> </div> </div> </div>For stabbing i was thinking about knife and blade in general, kevlar is great for stopping bullet and shrapnels bus isn't efficient for stopping a knife, mostly because of it's conception. Hard plate is good for both projectile and blade, but it depend a lot of the material and how thick it is, wich is why there is 6 level of body armor in us army. Low powered weapon are mostly handguns, pistol, revolver and low caliber. High powered weapon are 357 magnum, shotguns and rifles (of course it's more complicated than that but for game purpose let's keep it simple), The ST can also define himself what weapon can be considered high powered and low powered according to real things or according to more &quot;cinematic&quot; conception<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550030#post1550030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is conversion total or partial?</div> </div> </div> </div>Total is the base, unless the amount of projectile (a full auto shot or shrapnels) is enough to hits unprotected part, but the st could also tell &quot;with 3/4 success on the attack roll the armor does not convert&quot; in this case. The metis gift &quot;Gift of the Porcupine&quot; use the success of the attack roll to determine if the attacker hurt himself on the sharp fur, so it can work like that for shooting<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550030#post1550030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How are you going to compensate for the increased incentive to use targeted shots to circumvent armor as you make armor stronger, as currently those penalties aren't that high as called shots aren't optimal for dealing damage and more oriented around secondary effects?</div> </div> </div> </div>Isn't the whole thing about firearms being about saving ammunitions by killing quickly the target instead of shooting without thinking? That problem already exist with actual rules, may the armor convert damage or not, and being a precise shooter is actually more accurate to reality since you don't have infinite ammo and must spare them when on the battlefield<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550030#post1550030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The WoD combat system is complicated. If you change one part, you impact dozens of others (and even more once you start looking at supernatural powers). That's why I said a full fix would take a lot more work, but still offered something to consider as a patch.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not scared of that kind of work, I've always liked to think outside the box to make my game unique and to make it fit how we like to play, after all the golden rule say &quot;Change some rules or don't use them as long as you enjoy the game&quot;, it come with good and bad side. If i have to completely re-write the book so that we can enjoy our story at best, i say let's f*cking go","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 04-11-2025, 06:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-14T11:23:58-07:00","id":1550115,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550060#post1550060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I would say that it does not convert explosion damage since shrapnels can fly in every direction including non armored part of the character</div> </div> </div> </div>So armor made <b>specifically</b> to reduce shrapnel doesn't do what it is meant to do?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550060#post1550060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isn't the whole thing about firearms being about saving ammunitions by killing quickly the target instead of shooting without thinking? That problem already exist with actual rules, may the armor convert damage or not, and being a precise shooter is actually more accurate to reality since you don't have infinite ammo and must spare them when on the battlefield</div> </div> </div> </div>First, not exactly. Using guns is, most of the time, not about being a precise shooter. At least not more than <b>absolutely any weapon ever</b>.<br /> <br /> Second, this is not what the system is about. On most RPGs normal attacks already consider that an attacker is focusing on being effective. However &quot;realistic&quot; the rules are is another matter entirely, but they are already modeling reasonable combat.<br /> <br /> The rule for Called Shots is meant to model the effects of crippling a specific part of the body, not to say they're the only mean to hit that body part. It exists simply because the game has no other rule able to dictate what exactly you hit and the effects thereof.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550060#post1550060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not scared of that kind of work, I've always liked to think outside the box to make my game unique and to make it fit how we like to play, after all the golden rule say &quot;Change some rules or don't use them as long as you enjoy the game&quot;, it come with good and bad side. If i have to completely re-write the book so that we can enjoy our story at best, i say let's f*cking go</div> </div> </div> </div>The point wasn't to scare you off of that work, but to show how much work it really was. It is a lot, because this change in rules will affect a lot of other rules. It <b>will</b> be a complete overhaul of the rules, and that generally means the work is either too much for little pay-off, the result is unplayable, and/or the current system simply isn't the best starting point for your goal.<br /> <br /> It seems to me that you would be better served by another game, actually. It is perfectly possible to run WoD on GURPS, which is made to be more precisely attuned and model the target realism enjoyed by each group.<br /> <br /> WoD is simply a bad system, and D&amp;D isn't by any means the only other option.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-14T16:40:52-07:00","id":1550124,"title":"","content":"These armor-rules are 3rd edition, but, they are new to me! For handling mortal-combat, I like 'em, but, for NPCs and at times, vampires play their characters when they are mortal, I like to the old-system, where you could soak non-agg. with your stamina (also, there was no bruise-damage). It kinda takes the fun away from agg-damage on mortals, <br /> <br /> For a lethal-weapon to bypass armor, I'd say, the armor soak-roll, the bullet/arrow/spear/etc. found a weak-spot or hit an uncovered-area. Not all 9mm rounds are going to hit the center of the chest where the Kevlar is; at times, a bullet will strike an arm, or a leg, or a tentacle. <br /> <br /> Think of it more of an abstract way to resolve combat that lets the ST decide where and how you got hit. Other games, like Cyperpunk: 2020, have precise hit-locations you roll and armor, with applied stopping-power vs. bullet-damage roll, so the GM doesn't have to think.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-14T17:29:11-07:00","id":1550133,"title":"","content":"To just put in a bit of real world thing into all of that... guns aren't about being accurate and saving ammunition when it comes to being weapons of war. For hunting or sport shooting that might be the case. In battle, guns are the dominant weapon because they don't require nearly as much skill or physical conditioning as older weapons to provide the same level of lethality. You can carry far more ammunition, that both wrecks hit targets and punctures armor with more ease than bows or cross bows, and can be fired fast enough that they remain functional even in relatively close quarters. Most estimates of modern (WWI and later) warfare put it around ~10K bullets fired for every enemy causality. Largely because machine guns are a thing, and machine guns have killed multiple orders of magnitude more people than snipers have. Bullets are, ultimately, cheap. Unloading 20 to kill one target quickly is more efficient than ensuring one shot one kill.<br /> <br /> This is why shrapnel spewing weapons are so brutal (and so many are banned by the Geneva conventions), and the last century has literally given people lead poisoning because so many bullets are in the soil from major battles and are slowly leaching lead into the ground water.<br /> <br /> Whether or not you want your RPG to reflect that is a different matter, but if you're looking at the real world and trying to make a game that has some sense of &quot;real&quot; that's the real you have to consider. The default way guns are used in violence against other humans is shooting a lot of bullets and hoping one hits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-18T08:27:39-07:00","id":1550238,"title":"","content":"Hmm, all that seems fair, would an bigger soak pool for armor a better way to represent how they work and make them more usefull in a gun fight situation? Or use minimal success on the attack roll to keep the damage lethal?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 04-18-2025, 11:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-18T15:33:23-07:00","id":1550250,"title":"","content":"As I said from the start, &quot;better&quot; for me would take a much larger and holistic approach to the combat engine of the game.<br /> <br /> A very specific issue that I already brought up, is that buffing armor while keeping the ability to target unarmored areas is going to negatively impact game play. This is exactly what happened in the nWoD/CofD 1e rules. Called shots and armors imparted the same kind of penalties, so it was very simple for players to figure out that if you can shoot someone wearing a Kevlar vest in the arm for a -2 penalty, or just shoot them normally for a -3 penalty... you always go for the arm/leg/etc. There was zero downside (and if you rolled well shooting someone in the arms or legs gets you extra impact too).<br /> <br /> The WoD math is more opaque and harder to know exactly when aiming for unarmored body parts is mathematically superior to not doing so, but if you do something like jack up the number of soak dice the armor provides, then you're increasing the net positives to just aiming around armor; which I pointed out isn't how fights really play out in a gun totting violent scenario.<br /> <br /> That isn't necessarily bad. In lots of genres the protagonists rarely bother with armor in stark defiance of real world combat logic, while heavily armored foes are easily dispatched by the protagonists unrealistic combat tactics that work because the intent is to make the protagonists look cool as they pull off wild combat stunts.<br /> <br /> So &quot;better&quot; is subjective to intent. If your intent is to get players to invest in armor, you need to make sure armor is valuable. Value isn't just the direct benefits, but how things slot into the total equation. If armor makes a character nigh invincible against direct attacks, but can be circumvented with a +1 difficulty to the attack roll, than the value of armor is really +1 difficultly to attacks instead of insane damage reduction; minus any penalties you might get for wearing armor (both mechanical and narrative).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-18T16:03:33-07:00","id":1550251,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550250#post1550250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I said from the start, &quot;better&quot; for me would take a much larger and holistic approach to the combat engine of the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then let's say different instead of better and speaking of different...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550250#post1550250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Value isn't just the direct benefits, but how things slot into the total equation. If armor makes a character nigh invincible against direct attacks, but can be circumvented with a +1 difficulty to the attack roll, than the value of armor is really +1 difficultly to attacks instead of insane damage reduction; minus any penalties you might get for wearing armor (both mechanical and narrative).</div> </div> </div> </div>So if instead of using damage reduction we increase the attack difficulty (wich is something used in Baldur's Gates 3 game if i'm not mistaken) you avoid the soak part simply by saying that it's harder to inflict damage. I think i recall an old Arhoun gift called &quot;aegis&quot; that act as a shield by increasing the attack difficulty to hit the user. I just think that this option is too punitive, especially when knowing that movements and cover also increase the attack diff. Maybe if the armor value is a success number to overcome to hit, like if the defenser is trying to dodge it can work better?<br /> If wearing an armor with a protection level of 3 then the attacker must roll at least 4 success to inflict damage, if the defenser is dodging he adds his protection score to it's dodging success, and there's no direct damage reduction for armor?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-18T23:58:11-07:00","id":1550258,"title":"","content":"Maybe? What's the point? What's the goal? What are you doing to make armor not go from kinda weak in the system to binary of &quot;completely defeats some enemies and is completely worthless against others?&quot;<br /> <br /> It really helps to know what you're trying to do before you can find the right mechanical solutions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-19T05:42:00-07:00","id":1550262,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550258#post1550258\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe? What's the point? What's the goal? What are you doing to make armor not go from kinda weak in the system to binary of &quot;completely defeats some enemies and is completely worthless against others?&quot;<br /> <br /> It really helps to know what you're trying to do before you can find the right mechanical solutions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Make them at least a problem for pc, when the vampire party are chased by vampire hunters fully equiped the one with celerity will have a huge advantage to overcome 3/4/5 armor soaking dice, making regular humans, even well equiped, not really dangerous. Same problem the other way if they face a kindred with Protean 2 or a werewolf that can deal aggr with their bare claws and fangs and don't have fortitude, a 5 dice armor will soak 2/3 damage at diff 6 in the average while with enough success and strength they would suffer 5/6 damage and get killed in 1 or 2 turn depending on their luck.<br /> <br /> The point of giving a slight boost to armor is to give a bit of a challenge for pc and a slightly better protection. I actually like the difficulty modifier for armor because it will decrease the amount of success one could get to overcome it, reducing attack's effectivness, and you can still reduce or suppress that modifier if using a power, one like wind claws gift would suppress it for exemple","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 04-19-2025, 08:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-19T11:40:54-07:00","id":1550274,"title":"","content":"OK, so, for me, the &quot;best&quot; way to deal with that as phrased is to get away from NPCs and PCs operating on the same rules. Most designers focus on the benefits of simplifying NPCs for STs(/GM/DM/etc.), but it also lets you pull tricks that make the enemies easier to tweak to match the level of threat you want without mucking with the combat rules as much.<br /> <br /> For example, if you have NPCs not roll damage or soak, how you calculate their static damage and soak values is something you can play around with that doesn't change the math as much as if you broadly change the combat system.<br /> <br /> So, things like damage type reductions (lethal to bashing) really doesn't matter to making human hunters more threatening. 8 Bashing vs. 8 Lethal is about if they survive not if they can keep fighting in the moment.<br /> <br /> If you set the calculation of NPC automatic soak value to: Stamina/2 + Armor (if applicable for either if you want to mirror the damage type divide), a Stamina 4 hunter decked out in high end armed police (SWAT, etc.) gear rated at 4 soaks 6 damage against instead of 4ish against Bashing and 2ish against Lethal/Agg. Not enough to completely tank what a vampire or werewolf can do, but enough to take a few hits without being out of the fight before they can do anything. You can do similar with NPC weapons to make high end gear more impactful and stress that getting into fights with people bringing assault rifles is dangerous even for supernaturals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-19T14:23:43-07:00","id":1550280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1550014-reflexion-about-armor-protection?p=1550274#post1550274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you set the calculation of NPC automatic soak value to: Stamina/2 + Armor (if applicable for either if you want to mirror the damage type divide), a Stamina 4 hunter decked out in high end armed police (SWAT, etc.) gear rated at 4 soaks 6 damage against instead of 4ish against Bashing and 2ish against Lethal/Agg. Not enough to completely tank what a vampire or werewolf can do, but enough to take a few hits without being out of the fight before they can do anything. You can do similar with NPC weapons to make high end gear more impactful and stress that getting into fights with people bringing assault rifles is dangerous even for supernaturals.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fixed soaking value is something i've tried, looking for multiple solution can be usefull but it seems there's not much other way to make armor a bit more usefull","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}